## The desktop's browser and media player: Plasma's panel pins the browser,
## and mpv opens video and music from Dolphin. Game Mode's Apps tab no longer
## has tiles for either — Game Mode runs in the Plasma session, and these are a
## switch to the desktop away. Comments stay on their own lines: the rest of
## this file does the same, and it avoids depending on how mkarchiso strips
## trailing ones.
mpv
chromium

## What installs and updates apps. Discover (with Plasma, below) is the store,
## on Flathub: a Flatpak carries its own libraries, so installing one cannot
## drag the base system into the partial upgrade that "pacman -Sy something"
## invites. Discover brings archlinux-appstream-data with it.
## checkupdates (pacman-contrib) keeps a tile menu's "check for update" safe on
## native packages: it compares against a throwaway database instead of
## running pacman -Sy, which on Arch leaves the system one partial upgrade from
## a mismatched libc.
##
## GNOME Software, GNOME Settings and Thunar used to be here, as Game Mode's
## Store, Settings and Files tiles; the tiles went, and so did they.
flatpak
pacman-contrib

## Plasma: the session every login runs. Desktop Mode is Plasma as it is; Game
## Mode is the tile launcher open over it, with Plasma's panel still underneath.
## omni-session-select moves between the two.
##
## A lean set rather than plasma-meta: the shell and compositor, the three tray
## applets that matter (network, sound, bluetooth), power and display settings,
## System Settings, and one file manager and one terminal. The rest of KDE can
## come from the store like any other app. No display manager — tty1's
## autologin loop in .bash_profile starts the session instead.
##
## "dolphin" is KDE's file manager. The GameCube emulator is dolphin-emu, and
## the router runs that name, so the two do not collide.
##
## Discover is here because Plasma's panel pins it by default, and without it
## the second icon on a fresh desktop is a blank page that does nothing. That
## pin is compiled into the task manager, and Plasma offers no clean way to
## change a default panel's pins short of shipping a whole look-and-feel
## package. It is also simply the store a KDE desktop expects. It and Game
## Mode's GNOME Software share one Flatpak installation, so whatever either one
## installs, both show, and the store polkit rule already covers it because it
## matches Flatpak's actions rather than any particular app.
plasma-desktop
plasma-nm
plasma-pa
bluedevil
powerdevil
kscreen
systemsettings
kde-gtk-config
breeze-gtk
xdg-desktop-portal-kde
konsole
dolphin
discover
## What puts a password dialog on the desktop when something asks polkit —
## Discover installing a system package, or a settings page that changes the
## system, on an install whose account has a password. Named here rather than
## trusted to arrive as a dependency: without an agent those requests are
## refused with nothing on screen to say why. Game Mode, being the same
## Plasma session, uses it too.
polkit-kde-agent

## The sign-in screen, for machines installed with "sign in automatically"
## off. greetd asks PAM whether the password is right; cage is a kiosk
## compositor that shows exactly one full-screen app — OmniOS's own sign-in
## screen. Neither runs on the live image or on an install that signs in by
## itself: omni-install turns them on only where a password is asked for.
greetd
cage

## Bluetooth, which is how a controller actually connects.
##
## A DualSense works over USB with nothing added — hid-playstation is in the
## kernel and steam-devices already carries the udev rules for 054c:0ce6, so
## SDL gets at the hidraw node and the full feature set with it. Wireless is
## the half that needs a stack, and without one a PS5 pad simply cannot be
## paired at all, which is how most people use them.
bluez
bluez-utils

## Branding — Phase 11
plymouth
ttf-dejavu
noto-fonts
noto-fonts-emoji

## Controllers. multilib ships no lib32 SDL2 of any kind; lib32-sdl3 is the
## only 32-bit SDL there, and sdl2-compat serves the SDL2 API on top of SDL3.
## game-devices-udev is AUR-only and lives in packages.aur.txt.
sdl2-compat
sdl3
lib32-sdl3
